    "id": "copper-wire-upgrade-caused-nationwide-fl-42edab51",
    "title": "Copper wire upgrade caused nationwide flight suspension, US transport secretary says",
    "titleJa": "Copper wire upgrade caused nationwide flight suspension, US transport secretary says",
    "summaryJa": "The US government is trying to find out whether the chaos was caused by \"incompetence\" or faulty equipment.",
    "bodyOriginal": "Copper wire upgrade caused nationwide flight suspension, US transport secretary says\n- Published\nA nationwide US ground stop that caused thousands of flight cancellations was caused by an issue following a copper wire upgrade, Transport Secretary Sean Duffy said.\nDuffy told a news conference that the government was trying to find out whether the problem was caused by \"incompetence\" on the part of the company doing the upgrades, or faulty equipment.\nThe US government does not believe it was as a result of a cyber-attack, although an investigation is still under way, Duffy said.\nFriday's suspension was the longest such incident since the 11 September 2001 attacks on the US and the disruption is expected to last for days.\nMaintenance was being carried out to switch from copper wire to fibre by a contractor called L3Harris, a technology provider company, Duffy said.\nThe US has \"an incredibly old telecommunications system\", he added.\n\"It was such an old wire that it was being discontinued,\" he said.\nThe transport secretary explained that a router that was being worked on had started flooding other air traffic control systems and created communication issues with planes which led the government to shut down the airspace for safety reasons.\nBryan Bedford, who heads the Federal Aviation Administration (FAA), told the same news conference: \"We see zero evidence of anything nefarious, and zero evidence of anything AI related.\"\nOn Saturday L3Harris said it had completed over 70% of the work to modernise communications infrastructure before the shutdown.\n\"We are supporting the FAA in its investigation to understand the conditions that led to the 13 November ground stop of our nation's air travel,\" it said in a statement.\n\"L3Harris takes the nationwide ground stop and its impact on travellers, airlines and air traffic operations seriously.\"\nNo flights were allowed to take off from the continental US for more than four hours from 15:54 EST (19:54 GMT) on Friday.\nMore than 3,500 flights were cancelled and more than 2,000 delayed in the US on Saturday, according to tracking website Flight Aware, as knock-on disruption continues.\nIn some cases, passengers were left stuck inside planes on the tarmac, unable to take off or to return to the gates. Some international flights were allowed to depart US airports, in an apparent effort to clear space.\nDuffy warned that a similar issue could arise again unless the US Congress approved upgrades to air traffic control infrastructure.\nUS President Donald Trump has requested $30.1bn for the upgrades. Both chambers of Congress are currently controlled by the Republican Party.\nFormer TSA Administrator John Pistole told the BBC that \"the fact that they were able to get it restarted in four-and-a-half hours is good news - it shows a resilience in the system - but what's to say it's not going to happen tomorrow?\"\nA nationwide ground stop has only been ordered one other time since all flights were banned from take-off for several days following the 11 September 2001 attacks.\nRelated topics\n- Published2 hours ago",
    "bodyJa": "Copper wire upgrade caused nationwide flight suspension, US transport secretary says\n- Published\nA nationwide US ground stop that caused thousands of flight cancellations was caused by an issue following a copper wire upgrade, Transport Secretary Sean Duffy said.\nDuffy told a news conference that the government was trying to find out whether the problem was caused by \"incompetence\" on the part of the company doing the upgrades, or faulty equipment.\nThe US government does not believe it was as a result of a cyber-attack, although an investigation is still under way, Duffy said.\nFriday's suspension was the longest such incident since the 11 September 2001 attacks on the US and the disruption is expected to last for days.\nMaintenance was being carried out to switch from copper wire to fibre by a contractor called L3Harris, a technology provider company, Duffy said.\nThe US has \"an incredibly old telecommunications system\", he added.\n\"It was such an old wire that it was being discontinued,\" he said.\nThe transport secretary explained that a router that was being worked on had started flooding other air traffic control systems and created communication issues with planes which led the government to shut down the airspace for safety reasons.\nBryan Bedford, who heads the Federal Aviation Administration (FAA), told the same news conference: \"We see zero evidence of anything nefarious, and zero evidence of anything AI related.\"\nOn Saturday L3Harris said it had completed over 70% of the work to modernise communications infrastructure before the shutdown.\n\"We are supporting the FAA in its investigation to understand the conditions that led to the 13 November ground stop of our nation's air travel,\" it said in a statement.\n\"L3Harris takes the nationwide ground stop and its impact on travellers, airlines and air traffic operations seriously.\"\nNo flights were allowed to take off from the continental US for more than four hours from 15:54 EST (19:54 GMT) on Friday.\nMore than 3,500 flights were cancelled and more than 2,000 delayed in the US on Saturday, according to tracking website Flight Aware, as knock-on disruption continues.\nIn some cases, passengers were left stuck inside planes on the tarmac, unable to take off or to return to the gates. Some international flights were allowed to depart US airports, in an apparent effort to clear space.\nDuffy warned that a similar issue could arise again unless the US Congress approved upgrades to air traffic control infrastructure.\nUS President Donald Trump has requested $30.1bn for the upgrades. Both chambers of Congress are currently controlled by the Republican Party.\nFormer TSA Administrator John Pistole told the BBC that \"the fact that they were able to get it restarted in four-and-a-half hours is good news - it shows a resilience in the system - but what's to say it's not going to happen tomorrow?\"\nA nationwide ground stop has only been ordered one other time since all flights were banned from take-off for several days following the 11 September 2001 attacks.\nRelated topics\n- Published2 hours ago",
    "source": "BBC",
    "sourceUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c6wy9lynq4qno?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
    "publishedAt": "2026-11-14T19:12:25+00:00",
    "category": "テクノロジー",
    "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/standard/240/cpsprodpb/ea11/live/3adfb390-e05f-11f1-9e26-b3b65586122f.jpg",
